@page "/docs/concepts/tokens"
@layout DocsLayout
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>Tokens — Vaultex</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-eyebrow">Concepts</div>
<h1>Tokens</h1>
<p class="docs-lead">
    Vaultex issues three kinds of tokens. Each has a job, a lifetime, and rules about where it can
    travel. This page explains what's inside, how to validate them, and when to ask for new ones.
</p>

<h2 id="three-tokens">The three tokens</h2>
<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Format</th><th>Audience</th><th>Lifetime</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>ID token</td><td>JWT</td><td>Your client app</td><td>1 hour</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Access token</td><td>JWT</td><td>Your APIs</td><td>1 hour</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refresh token</td><td>Opaque</td><td>Vaultex only</td><td>14 days, rotates on use</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="id-token">ID token</h2>
<p>
    The ID token is how Vaultex tells your app who the user is. It's a signed JWT with claims
    describing the user. Your client validates the signature and reads the claims &mdash; that's it.
    ID tokens never travel to your APIs.
</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">JSON (decoded payload)</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>{
  "iss":   "https://auth.vaultex.io",
  "aud":   "your-client-id",
  "sub":   "f3a7d2c9-...",
  "name":  "Sarah Chen",
  "email": "sarah@example.com",
  "tid":   "acme",
  "iat":   1715900400,
  "exp":   1715904000,
  "nonce": "n-0S6_..."
}</code></pre>
</div>

<h2 id="access-token">Access token</h2>
<p>
    The access token is what your app sends to your APIs. It's a JWT signed by Vaultex with claims
    about <em>what the user is allowed to do</em>: roles, scopes, tenant. Your APIs validate it on
    every request.
</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">HTTP</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>GET /api/orders HTTP/1.1
Host: api.yourapp.com
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...</code></pre>
</div>

<h2 id="refresh-token">Refresh token</h2>
<p>
    Refresh tokens let your backend exchange one expired session for a new one without bothering
    the user. They're long-lived and powerful, so treat them like passwords:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Store on the server, never in the browser.</li>
    <li>Send only to <code>/connect/token</code> with <code>grant_type=refresh_token</code>.</li>
    <li>Vaultex returns a <em>new</em> refresh token each time &mdash; the old one becomes invalid.</li>
</ul>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warn">
    <span class="docs-callout-icon">
        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M1 21h22L12 2 1 21zm12-3h-2v-2h2v2zm0-4h-2v-4h2v4z"/></svg>
    </span>
    <div class="docs-callout-body">
        <div class="docs-callout-title">Refresh-token rotation</div>
        <p>If a leaked refresh token is reused after rotation, Vaultex revokes the entire token chain. Your user is logged out, which is exactly the right outcome.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="validate">Validating an access token</h2>
<p>API services should validate every incoming access token. Modern frameworks do this for you:</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">C#</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>builder.Services.AddAuthentication("Bearer")
    .AddJwtBearer("Bearer", o =&gt;
    {
        o.Authority = "https://auth.vaultex.io";
        o.Audience  = "your-api-resource";
        o.TokenValidationParameters.ValidateIssuer = true;
    });</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
    The library fetches Vaultex's public signing keys from the JWKS endpoint and caches them. Every
    incoming request is verified against those keys.
</p>

